<p data-start=3D"1205" data-end=3D"1354">This split is consistent with NIST=
=E2=80=99s distinction between Type B and Type C, and GS1/RAIN=E2=80=99s po=
sitioning of Gen2 as the core modern UHF RFID standard.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"1356" data-end=3D"1359">
<h2 data-section-id=3D"v5winh" data-start=3D"1361" data-end=3D"1383">3. ISO=
/IEC 18000-6B</h2>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"3h1ku8" data-start=3D"1385" data-end=3D"1400">Descri=
ption</h3>
<p data-start=3D"1401" data-end=3D"1682"><strong data-start=3D"1401" data-e=
nd=3D"1421">ISO/IEC 18000-6B</strong> is an older UHF RFID air-interface st=
andard. It is still relevant mainly where older tags or older installed sys=
tems are already in use. NIST identifies Type B as a separate branch from T=
ype C, with different forward-link encoding and anti-collision behavior.</p=
>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"1ji6rz7" data-start=3D"1684" data-end=3D"1708">Main =
characteristics</h3>
<ul data-start=3D"1709" data-end=3D"1966">
<li data-section-id=3D"nbva6a" data-start=3D"1709" data-end=3D"1828">
<p data-start=3D"1711" data-end=3D"1828">Reader initiates communication and=
 passive tags respond by backscatter, as part of the ISO/IEC 18000-6 family=
 model.</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1u78efd" data-start=3D"1829" data-end=3D"1900">
<p data-start=3D"1831" data-end=3D"1900">Used mainly for <strong data-start=
=3D"1847" data-end=3D"1874">legacy interoperability</strong> rather than ne=
w designs.</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"dxqdwu" data-start=3D"1901" data-end=3D"1966">
<p data-start=3D"1903" data-end=3D"1966">Smaller ecosystem of tags and solu=
tions compared with 6C/Gen2.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"135aw7p" data-start=3D"1968" data-end=3D"1992">Typic=
al applications</h3>
<ul data-start=3D"1993" data-end=3D"2109">
<li data-section-id=3D"p7zt2o" data-start=3D"1993" data-end=3D"2024">
<p data-start=3D"1995" data-end=3D"2024">Older industrial RFID systems</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"nprii7" data-start=3D"2025" data-end=3D"2048">
<p data-start=3D"2027" data-end=3D"2048">Legacy access systems</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"otflma" data-start=3D"2049" data-end=3D"2109">
<p data-start=3D"2051" data-end=3D"2109">Environments where installed 6B ta=
gs must continue to work</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"2111" data-end=3D"2114">
<h2 data-section-id=3D"rj10vf" data-start=3D"2116" data-end=3D"2138">4. ISO=
/IEC 18000-6C</h2>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"3h1ku8" data-start=3D"2140" data-end=3D"2155">Descri=
ption</h3>
<p data-start=3D"2156" data-end=3D"2455"><strong data-start=3D"2156" data-e=
nd=3D"2176">ISO/IEC 18000-6C</strong> is the widely adopted modern UHF RFID=
 air-interface standard, commonly known as <strong data-start=3D"2257" data=
-end=3D"2278">EPC Class 1 Gen 2</strong>. GS1 describes Gen2 as the protoco=
l defining the physical and logical requirements for UHF RFID systems, and =
the RAIN Alliance ties modern deployments to <strong data-start=3D"2433" da=
ta-end=3D"2453">ISO/IEC 18000-63</strong>.</p>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"1ji6rz7" data-start=3D"2457" data-end=3D"2481">Main =
characteristics</h3>
<ul data-start=3D"2482" data-end=3D"2769">
<li data-section-id=3D"a0w2a8" data-start=3D"2482" data-end=3D"2561">
<p data-start=3D"2484" data-end=3D"2561">Reader powers passive tag and send=
s commands; tag replies using backscatter.</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1mi5gm" data-start=3D"2562" data-end=3D"2647">
<p data-start=3D"2564" data-end=3D"2647">Designed for <strong data-start=3D=
"2577" data-end=3D"2612">high-volume item identification</strong> and <stro=
ng data-start=3D"2617" data-end=3D"2645">multi-tag anti-collision</strong>.=
</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"nk20v8" data-start=3D"2648" data-end=3D"2721">
<p data-start=3D"2650" data-end=3D"2721">Broad interoperability across read=
ers, tags, printers, and middleware.</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"9wrxk7" data-start=3D"2722" data-end=3D"2769">
<p data-start=3D"2724" data-end=3D"2769">Dominant choice for modern UHF RFI=
D systems.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"135aw7p" data-start=3D"2771" data-end=3D"2795">Typic=
al applications</h3>
<ul data-start=3D"2796" data-end=3D"2923">
<li data-section-id=3D"1jsfphh" data-start=3D"2796" data-end=3D"2814">
<p data-start=3D"2798" data-end=3D"2814">Retail inventory</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"s9s1hf" data-start=3D"2815" data-end=3D"2848">
<p data-start=3D"2817" data-end=3D"2848">Logistics and warehouse systems</p=
>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1eqi4uv" data-start=3D"2849" data-end=3D"2865">
<p data-start=3D"2851" data-end=3D"2865">Asset tracking</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1r3qroa" data-start=3D"2866" data-end=3D"2890">
<p data-start=3D"2868" data-end=3D"2890">Vehicle identification</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1bny23q" data-start=3D"2891" data-end=3D"2923">
<p data-start=3D"2893" data-end=3D"2923">Modern gate/access UHF systems</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"2925" data-end=3D"2928">
<h2 data-section-id=3D"1omdkkp" data-start=3D"2930" data-end=3D"2967">5. Pr=
actical Meaning for HX-R01B-9</h2>
<p data-start=3D"2969" data-end=3D"3072">If the <strong data-start=3D"2976"=
 data-end=3D"2989">HX-R01B-9</strong> supports <strong data-start=3D"2999" =
data-end=3D"3022">6B/6C dual protocol</strong>, that means the reader can l=
ikely work with both:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"3073" data-end=3D"3122">
<li data-section-id=3D"1tszgo8" data-start=3D"3073" data-end=3D"3093">
<p data-start=3D"3075" data-end=3D"3093"><strong data-start=3D"3075" data-e=
nd=3D"3093">legacy 6B tags</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1ym9mk2" data-start=3D"3094" data-end=3D"3122">
<p data-start=3D"3096" data-end=3D"3122"><strong data-start=3D"3096" data-e=
nd=3D"3121">modern 6C / Gen2 tags</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"3124" data-end=3D"3288">For a <strong data-start=3D"3130" =
data-end=3D"3145">new project</strong>, the normal recommendation is to cho=
ose <strong data-start=3D"3186" data-end=3D"3204">6C / Gen2 tags</strong>, =
because the ecosystem is larger and the protocol is the dominant modern sta=
ndard.</p>
