<p data-start=3D"7178" data-end=3D"7228">A directional coupler is a four-po=
rt network with:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"7229" data-end=3D"7282">
<li data-section-id=3D"13k5oe6" data-start=3D"7229" data-end=3D"7240">
<p data-start=3D"7231" data-end=3D"7240"><strong data-start=3D"7231" data-e=
nd=3D"7240">input</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"3bu939" data-start=3D"7241" data-end=3D"7253">
<p data-start=3D"7243" data-end=3D"7253"><strong data-start=3D"7243" data-e=
nd=3D"7253">direct</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"10ttaa4" data-start=3D"7254" data-end=3D"7267">
<p data-start=3D"7256" data-end=3D"7267"><strong data-start=3D"7256" data-e=
nd=3D"7267">coupled</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"ld6hlx" data-start=3D"7268" data-end=3D"7282">
<p data-start=3D"7270" data-end=3D"7282"><strong data-start=3D"7270" data-e=
nd=3D"7282">isolated</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"7284" data-end=3D"7415">It samples power traveling in one =
direction more strongly than the other. In this reader architecture it serv=
es two major purposes:</p>
<ol data-start=3D"7417" data-end=3D"7710">
<li data-section-id=3D"b5d81e" data-start=3D"7417" data-end=3D"7520">
<p data-start=3D"7420" data-end=3D"7520"><strong data-start=3D"7420" data-e=
nd=3D"7437">TX monitoring</strong><br data-start=3D"7437" data-end=3D"7440"=
>
A small fraction of forward transmit power is sampled and sent to a detecto=
r.</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1rxyrsj" data-start=3D"7522" data-end=3D"7710">
<p data-start=3D"7525" data-end=3D"7710"><strong data-start=3D"7525" data-e=
nd=3D"7545">TX/RX separation</strong><br data-start=3D"7545" data-end=3D"75=
48">
The coupler helps route the large transmit carrier and the weak receive bac=
kscatter differently, so the RX input is less overwhelmed by the transmitte=
d signal.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h3 data-section-id=3D"wgwuyd" data-start=3D"7712" data-end=3D"7730">Why it=
 matters</h3>
<p data-start=3D"7731" data-end=3D"7759">This block directly affects:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"7760" data-end=3D"7845">
<li data-section-id=3D"yx6yf9" data-start=3D"7760" data-end=3D"7773">
<p data-start=3D"7762" data-end=3D"7773">sensitivity</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1tmz4oi" data-start=3D"7774" data-end=3D"7788">
<p data-start=3D"7776" data-end=3D"7788">self-jamming</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1i6y7zj" data-start=3D"7789" data-end=3D"7808">
<p data-start=3D"7791" data-end=3D"7808">detector accuracy</p>
</li>
<li data-section-id=3D"1oaz7uw" data-start=3D"7809" data-end=3D"7845">
<p data-start=3D"7811" data-end=3D"7845">carrier-cancellation effectiveness=
</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"7847" data-end=3D"8171">Bad coupler parasitics or poor lay=
out here can destroy the receiver=E2=80=99s performance even if the PA powe=
r looks good. ST says the self-jamming signal is made up of <strong data-st=
art=3D"8008" data-end=3D"8037">antenna reflections (S11)</strong> plus <str=
ong data-start=3D"8043" data-end=3D"8090">leakage across the main direction=
al coupler</strong>, which is why this block is so critical. <span class=3D=
